{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -C 3 'REAPER|ReaScript|verify_phase|reference_index|Main_OnCommand|defer' docs scripts tests README.md && find /Applications/REAPER.app/Contents -iname '*reascript*' -o -iname '*reaper*api*' | head -80 && sed -n '1,220p' docs/phase0-live-verification.md && sed -n '1,160p' README.md\"","aggregated_output":"README.md-1-# vgt\nREADME.md-2-\nREADME.md:3:Phase 0 of virtual guitar teacher: safe REAPER project plumbing. It reads an RPP project from the command line and uses a REAPER ReaScript action to add a small, vgt-owned practice area.\nREADME.md-4-\nREADME.md-5-## Install and inspect\nREADME.md-6-\n--\nREADME.md-13-\nREADME.md-14-`vgt [project.rpp]` (or the explicit `vgt inspect [project.rpp]`) accepts an explicit `.RPP` path. Without one it selects the only `.RPP` in the current directory; it refuses to guess if there are zero or multiple projects. It reports the project's sample rate, tempo/time signature, and track names/GUIDs.\nREADME.md-15-\nREADME.md:16:## Apply inside REAPER\nREADME.md-17-\nREADME.md:18:Open and save the target project in REAPER 7.x. In **Actions → Show action list → ReaScript: Load**, load [reascript/vgt_initialize.lua](reascript/vgt_initialize.lua), then run it.\nREADME.md-19-\nREADME.md:20:The action is intentionally the mutation path: it uses REAPER's API and never text-edits an RPP. It:\nREADME.md-21-\nREADME.md-22-- pops up a menu of the project's own (non-`[vgt]`) tracks and asks which one is the **reference** to mirror;\nREADME.md:23:- creates a REAPER folder named `[vgt] <reference track name>` (e.g. `[vgt] The Seven Rivers (Full March - 3_00)`) with a `[vgt] Mirror` child;\nREADME.md-24-- clones only the chosen reference track's file-backed media to the mirror track, without adding sends, changing source tracks, or changing project tempo/sample rate;\nREADME.md-25-- writes the adjacent `.vgt` sidecar — named after the project, e.g. `Reaper Project.vgt` — with its two created track GUIDs and config recording the reference track;\nREADME.md-26-- on re-apply, deletes only tracks whose GUID occurs in the sidecar **and** whose current name begins `[vgt]`, then recreates the same area, carrying forward any `analysis` block `vgt analyze` had already written (schema stays version 1 until analysis exists, then becomes version 2).\nREADME.md-27-\nREADME.md:28:The folder/mirror are therefore idempotent and original tracks remain untouched. REAPER-native click-source items have no file to clone, so choosing a click track mirrors nothing. `vgt apply` validates the project path and directs you to this action rather than silently falling back to unsafe RPP editing.\nREADME.md-29-\nREADME.md:30:Automation can skip the menu by setting the `vgt`/`reference_index` ExtState (a 0-based index over the non-`[vgt]` tracks) before running the action; interactive users are always prompted.\nREADME.md-31-\nREADME.md-32-Use a copy of any project when experimenting; the included `test/Reaper Project` fixture is read-mostly.\nREADME.md-33-\nREADME.md:34:For a repeatable live-REAPER run against a disposable copy of that fixture, see\nREADME.md-35-[Phase 0 live verification](docs/phase0-live-verification.md). The verifier\nREADME.md-36-checks the saved RPP and sidecar after both the first apply and re-apply.\nREADME.md-37-\n--\nREADME.md-40-`vgt analyze [project.rpp]` requires a `.vgt` sidecar already written by the\nREADME.md-41-apply action above. It resolves the reference track's source audio file,\nREADME.md-42-runs the tempo/key/sections/chords detectors, and writes the results back\nREADME.md:43:into the sidecar as schema version 2 — never inside REAPER itself.\nREADME.md-44-\nREADME.md-45-Each detector's output is cached against a hash of the source audio and the\nREADME.md-46-detector's settings, so re-running only recomputes stages whose inputs\n--\nREADME.md-131-\nREADME.md-132-## Apply analysis (Phase 1)\nREADME.md-133-\nREADME.md:134:Run the same ReaScript again after `vgt analyze`. It reads the sidecar and adds\nREADME.md-135-`[vgt]` section regions plus a muted, locked `[vgt] Chords` track whose text\nREADME.md-136-items carry the detected beat-aligned labels. It sets vgt-owned audio items to\nREADME.md-137-time-based positioning before any tempo changes.\nREADME.md-138-\nREADME.md:139:When REAPER still has exactly its single default 120 BPM / 4/4 tempo marker,\nREADME.md-140-the action writes the detected tempo map. If the project already has any other\nREADME.md-141-tempo map, it leaves that map untouched and instead creates a muted, locked\nREADME.md-142-`[vgt] Beats` item track. Both the regions and vgt tracks are reconciled on\n--\nREADME.md-145-For a saved-project check after analysis, use:\nREADME.md-146-\nREADME.md-147-```sh\nREADME.md:148:uv run python scripts/verify_phase1_apply.py \"$PROJECT\" \\\nREADME.md-149-  --baseline \"test/Reaper Project/Reaper Project.RPP\"\nREADME.md-150-```\n--\ndocs/AGENTS.md-8-\ndocs/AGENTS.md-9-## Project overview\ndocs/AGENTS.md-10-\ndocs/AGENTS.md:11:**vgt** (virtual guitar teacher) is a CLI that prepares REAPER projects for\ndocs/AGENTS.md:12:guitar practice. This project is **phase 0**: locate/open a REAPER project,\ndocs/AGENTS.md-13-store vgt's own state next to it, create a `[vgt]`-managed area, and add one\ndocs/AGENTS.md-14-track that mirrors the original song — all non-destructively and idempotently.\ndocs/AGENTS.md-15-See `docs/GOAL.md` for the exact scope and `docs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md` for\n--\ndocs/AGENTS.md-17-\ndocs/AGENTS.md-18-## Environment assumptions\ndocs/AGENTS.md-19-\ndocs/AGENTS.md:20:- **REAPER is installed on the machine.** You may assume a working REAPER\ndocs/AGENTS.md-21-  install (target: 7.x; the test fixture was saved with 7.65) at the standard\ndocs/AGENTS.md:22:  macOS location `/Applications/REAPER.app`, with the CLI binary at\ndocs/AGENTS.md:23:  `/Applications/REAPER.app/Contents/MacOS/REAPER`. It is fine to invoke REAPER\ndocs/AGENTS.md:24:  (e.g. to run a ReaScript action) or rely on its presence in tests; you do not\ndocs/AGENTS.md-25-  need to bundle, download, or install it.\ndocs/AGENTS.md-26-- Platform is macOS (Apple Silicon). Python 3.11 + `uv` per the plan's stack.\ndocs/AGENTS.md-27-\ndocs/AGENTS.md-28-## Test project\ndocs/AGENTS.md-29-\ndocs/AGENTS.md:30:A real REAPER project fixture lives at **`test/Reaper Project/`** — use it for\ndocs/AGENTS.md-31-developing and testing project locating, reading, and non-destructive\ndocs/AGENTS.md-32-augmentation against a genuine `.RPP` (not a synthetic one).\ndocs/AGENTS.md-33-\ndocs/AGENTS.md:34:- `Reaper Project.RPP` — REAPER 7.74 project, 3 tracks: `Click`, and two song\ndocs/AGENTS.md-35-  tracks `The Seven Rivers (Full March - 3_00)` and `Paris Metro Punk`.\ndocs/AGENTS.md-36-- `Media/*.mp3` — the two song audio files, referenced by the `.RPP` via\ndocs/AGENTS.md-37-  **relative** paths (`FILE \"Media/...\"`), so they resolve regardless of\ndocs/AGENTS.md:38:  checkout location. `Click` is a REAPER-native click source with no file.\ndocs/AGENTS.md:39:- REAPER-generated peak caches (`Media/peaks/`, `*.reapeaks`) and auto-backups\ndocs/AGENTS.md:40:  (`Backups/`) are **git-ignored** — REAPER regenerates peaks on open, so don't\ndocs/AGENTS.md-41-  rely on them being present and don't commit them.\ndocs/AGENTS.md-42-\ndocs/AGENTS.md-43-Treat this as a **read-mostly fixture**: exercise vgt against a copy, and never\n--\ndocs/AGENTS.md-47-\ndocs/AGENTS.md-48-## Conventions\ndocs/AGENTS.md-49-\ndocs/AGENTS.md:50:- Everything vgt creates in a REAPER project carries a `[vgt]` prefix (tracks,\ndocs/AGENTS.md-51-  regions, folders); only `[vgt]`-owned objects may be modified or removed.\ndocs/AGENTS.md-52-- Commit style: conventional commits (see git history).\ndocs/AGENTS.md-53-\n--\ndocs/AGENTS.md-56-- **Non-destructive & idempotent** are hard invariants — never overwrite,\ndocs/AGENTS.md-57-  rename, or delete tracks/objects vgt didn't create; re-running a command must\ndocs/AGENTS.md-58-  not duplicate or corrupt anything.\ndocs/AGENTS.md:59:- Prefer manipulating the project through the REAPER API (ReaScript action) over\ndocs/AGENTS.md-60-  editing `.RPP` text, so changes appear live in the user's open project and\ndocs/AGENTS.md:61:  REAPER handles construction correctly.\n--\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py-1-#!/usr/bin/env python3\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py:2:\"\"\"Check the persisted result of a live Phase 0 REAPER apply.\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py-3-\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py:4:This intentionally reads the saved RPP and sidecar after REAPER has run the\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py:5:ReaScript.  It does not invoke REAPER or modify the project.\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py-6-\"\"\"\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py-7-\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py-8-from __future__ import annotations\n--\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py-22-\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py-23-PREFIX = \"[vgt]\"\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py-24-MIRROR_NAME = f\"{PREFIX} Mirror\"\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py:25:# REAPER persists the I_FOLDERDEPTH API value as the second ISBUS field in RPP.\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py-26-_FOLDER_DEPTH = re.compile(r\"^\\s*ISBUS\\s+\\d+\\s+(-?\\d+)\", re.MULTILINE)\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py-27-_FILE = re.compile(r'^\\s*FILE\\s+\"(?P<path>.*)\"', re.MULTILINE)\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py-28-\n--\ntests/test_reascript.py-3-import subprocess\ntests/test_reascript.py-4-\ntests/test_reascript.py-5-\ntests/test_reascript.py:6:VERIFY_SCRIPT = Path(__file__).parents[1] / \"scripts\" / \"verify_phase0_apply.py\"\ntests/test_reascript.py:7:PHASE1_VERIFY_SCRIPT = Path(__file__).parents[1] / \"scripts\" / \"verify_phase1_apply.py\"\ntests/test_reascript.py-8-APPLY_SCRIPT = Path(__file__).parents[1] / \"reascript\" / \"vgt_initialize.lua\"\ntests/test_reascript.py-9-\ntests/test_reascript.py-10-\n--\ntests/test_reascript.py-23-    script = APPLY_SCRIPT.read_text()\ntests/test_reascript.py-24-    # Interactive pick with a headless override, then the folder is named after the choice.\ntests/test_reascript.py-25-    assert \"gfx.showmenu\" in script\ntests/test_reascript.py:26:    assert 'reaper.GetExtState(\"vgt\", \"reference_index\")' in script\ntests/test_reascript.py-27-    assert 'PREFIX .. \" \" .. track_name(reference)' in script\ntests/test_reascript.py-28-    # Only the chosen reference is mirrored, not every track.\ntests/test_reascript.py-29-    assert \"copy_file_backed_items(reference, mirror)\" in script\n--\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-3-Status: proposed (rev 2, after discussion) · Date: 2026-07-18\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-4-Basis: [[Building a virtual guitar teacher]] and [[AI-assisted instrument practice and performance assessment]] in `topics/music-ai/wiki/` (and the two retained deep-research PDFs in `topics/music-ai/source/`).\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-5-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:6:Decisions from discussion: LALAL.AI (active subscription) is the default separator with Demucs as offline fallback; target machine is an M4 MacBook Air (16 GB, fanless); the primary workflow starts from a song already loaded in an **existing REAPER project**, so the importer augments the open project rather than generating a fresh one.\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-7-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-8-## Goal\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-9-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:10:Starting from a song item in an open REAPER project (or a bare audio file), produce in that project:\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-11-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-12-- separated stems on named `[vgt]` tracks (guitar isolated; backing = bass/drums/vocals/other),\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-13-- a tempo map and downbeat grid (only where the project doesn't already have one — see the tempo-map rule below),\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:14:- song sections as `[vgt]` REAPER regions (intro/verse/chorus/…),\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-15-- detected key and beat-aligned chords as text items on a muted \"[vgt] Chords\" track,\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-16-- a draft guitar reference as a muted MIDI track (with pitch bends),\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-17-- a record-armed \"MY TAKE\" track,\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-18-- a machine-readable `manifest.json` recording every tool, version, setting, and human correction.\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-19-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:20:**Non-goals for phase 1** (explicitly deferred): performance scoring, practice UI/looping logic (phase 2), tempo-ramped renders, tab generation (deprioritized in the source research as too error-prone to be the first investment), any hard cloud dependency — the default path uses LALAL.AI, but the tool must remain fully **offline-capable** via the Demucs fallback.\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-21-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-22-## Where the code lives\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-23-\n--\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-25-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-26-## Architecture\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-27-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:28:Two components, cleanly split — this mirrors the \"preparation may be heavy/offline, practice must be local/deterministic\" principle from the research. REAPER is the driver: the workflow starts and ends inside the user's open project.\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-29-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-30-```\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-31-┌─────────────────────────────────┐  launches   ┌────────────────────────────┐\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:32:│  REAPER (user's open project)   │  detached   │  vgt CLI (Python 3.11)     │\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-33-│                                 │────────────▶│  staged pipeline:          │\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-34-│  vgt_analyze.lua                │             │  ingest → separate (LALAL/ │\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-35-│   • selected item → source path │             │  Demucs) → beats → key →   │\n--\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-44-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-45-Rationale:\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-46-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:47:- **Heavy ML deps (PyTorch, ONNX) stay out of the DAW process.** The CLI is a batch tool; REAPER never loads torch.\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:48:- **No export step needed.** `vgt_analyze.lua` reads the selected item's *source file path* via the REAPER API and feeds it to the pipeline directly, recording the item's position offset for the import step. Edge case: if the item is trimmed or time-stretched, v1 refuses with a clear message (\"use the full, unstretched item\"); render-to-temp is the v2 fallback.\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:49:- **The project is augmented by REAPER**, via `vgt_apply.lua` reading `manifest.json` — not by writing `.RPP` text. The API route can't corrupt the project, handles tempo-map/region/item construction correctly, and the same manifest contract feeds phase 2's practice controller later.\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-50-- **Non-destructive and idempotent.** Everything vgt creates is `[vgt]`-prefixed (tracks, regions). Applying never deletes or renames anything it didn't create; re-applying first removes only its own `[vgt]` objects. All imported times are shifted by the analyzed item's position.\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-51-- **Tempo-map rule** (the one genuinely invasive write — a project has exactly one tempo map): write it only if the project still has a single default tempo marker; otherwise leave the map untouched and ask, offering a muted \"[vgt] beats\" marker-item track as the non-invasive alternative.\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-52-- **`manifest.json` is the contract.** Analysis is useless if it can't be corrected; every field is overridable and corrections survive re-runs.\n--\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-69-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-70-### Tempo-map construction (the subtle part)\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-71-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:72:Naively emitting one REAPER tempo marker per detected beat produces an unusable, jittery map. Instead:\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-73-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-74-1. Fit beat intervals; if variance is below a threshold, emit a **single constant BPM + downbeat offset** (covers most practice-worthy recordings, including anything cut to a click).\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-75-2. Otherwise fit **piecewise-linear tempo spans** and emit markers only at significant changes.\n--\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-117-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-118-## Stack and target hardware\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-119-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:120:Python 3.11 · `uv` for env/deps · `typer` CLI · `pydantic` manifest models · ffmpeg · LALAL.AI REST API (default separator) · torch (Demucs fallback; MPS) · ONNX Runtime (Basic Pitch) · Lua ReaScript with a vendored JSON decoder for the two REAPER actions.\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-121-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-122-Target machine: **M4 MacBook Air, 16 GB unified memory** (confirmed). Two consequences: it's fanless, so sustained local separation would thermal-throttle — another reason LALAL-by-default is right; and with separation offloaded, the remaining local stages (madmom, chord CNN, Basic Pitch) are lightweight, putting expected end-to-end time at ~2–3 min/song plus upload. Fully offline operation (Demucs fallback) remains supported, just slower and warmer.\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-123-\n--\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-141-- **Dependency fragility** (madmom pins, Essentia wheels, archived Demucs): mitigated by pinning, extras groups, and interface seams for each stage.\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-142-- **Analysis accuracy on real mixes** (chords/sections especially): mitigated by making the correction loop first-class and shipping verification artifacts, not by chasing model quality in phase 1.\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-143-- **Cloud in the default path**: LALAL API changes, credit exhaustion, or subscription lapse must degrade gracefully to Demucs — the fallback is exercised in tests, not just theoretical. Uploaded audio is the user's own, for personal practice.\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:144:- **Writing into a live user project**: the highest-consequence surface. Mitigated by the `[vgt]` prefix discipline, additive-only writes, idempotent re-apply, the tempo-map rule, and REAPER's undo block wrapping every apply.\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-145-- **Rights**: processing the user's own audio for personal practice; stems and manifests stay on-disk and are not redistributed — consistent with the provenance guidance in `Copyright, consent, and provenance in music AI`.\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-146-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-147-## Open questions\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-148-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-149-1. ~~Target machine?~~ Resolved: M4 MacBook Air, 16 GB. LALAL default; Demucs fallback on MPS.\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:150:2. REAPER version in use (importer targets 7.x APIs) — assumed 7.x, confirm.\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-151-3. First 3–5 golden songs — genre affects separation quality expectations and chord-vocabulary adequacy.\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-152-4. ~~LALAL.AI subscription?~~ Resolved: active — API adapter is the default separator, built in M1.\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-153-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-154-## Definition of done (phase 1)\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md-155-\ndocs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:156:On the target machine: open an existing REAPER project containing a song item → run `vgt: Analyze selected item` → pipeline completes (LALAL stems + local analysis) → `vgt: Apply results` augments the project per the tables above, non-destructively and idempotently; corrections in `corrections.yaml` survive re-runs; all analysis provenance is in `manifest.json`. Then phase 2 (practice controller: stem muting, loop/tempo management, take recording) builds on the same manifest and the same `[vgt]` track conventions.\n--\nscripts/verify_phase1_apply.py-1-#!/usr/bin/env python3\nscripts/verify_phase1_apply.py:2:\"\"\"Check the persisted result of a live Phase 1 REAPER apply.\nscripts/verify_phase1_apply.py-3-\nscripts/verify_phase1_apply.py:4:The verifier is deliberately read-only: REAPER performs every project mutation\nscripts/verify_phase1_apply.py-5-through ``reascript/vgt_initialize.lua`` and this script examines the saved RPP\nscripts/verify_phase1_apply.py-6-and its sidecar after first apply or re-apply.\nscripts/verify_phase1_apply.py-7-\"\"\"\n--\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md:1:# Phase 0 live REAPER verification\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-2-\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md:3:This procedure proves the persisted result of the ReaScript, rather than only\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-4-inspecting its Lua source. It uses a disposable copy and leaves the committed\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-5-fixture untouched.\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-6-\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-7-## Prerequisites\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-8-\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md:9:- macOS with REAPER 7.x at `/Applications/REAPER.app`.\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-10-- Python 3.11 and `uv`.\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md:11:- REAPER may already be open; the command below starts an isolated REAPER\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-12-  instance with `-newinst`.\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-13-\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-14-## Run\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-15-\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-16-From the repository root, make a temporary copy that preserves the relative\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-17-`Media/` directory. The helper save script is deliberately outside the repo;\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md:18:it makes REAPER save after the apply action.\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-19-\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-20-The apply action prompts for a reference track. To keep the run non-interactive,\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md:21:a `select.lua` runs first and sets the `vgt`/`reference_index` ExtState (0-based\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-22-over the project's non-`[vgt]` tracks); index `1` is the first song track after\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-23-`Click`.\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-24-\n--\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-28-PROJECT=\"$RUN_DIR/Reaper Project/Reaper Project.RPP\"\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-29-SELECT_SCRIPT=\"$RUN_DIR/select.lua\"\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-30-SAVE_SCRIPT=\"$RUN_DIR/save.lua\"\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md:31:printf '%s\\n' 'reaper.SetExtState(\"vgt\", \"reference_index\", \"1\", false)' > \"$SELECT_SCRIPT\"\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-32-printf '%s\\n' 'reaper.Main_SaveProject(0, false)' > \"$SAVE_SCRIPT\"\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-33-\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md:34:/Applications/REAPER.app/Contents/MacOS/REAPER -newinst \\\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-35-  \"$PROJECT\" \"$SELECT_SCRIPT\" \"$PWD/reascript/vgt_initialize.lua\" \"$SAVE_SCRIPT\"\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md:36:uv run python scripts/verify_phase0_apply.py \"$PROJECT\" \\\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-37-  --baseline \"test/Reaper Project/Reaper Project.RPP\"\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-38-\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-39-# Repeat the exact select/apply/save sequence, then check it again for idempotency.\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md:40:/Applications/REAPER.app/Contents/MacOS/REAPER -newinst \\\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-41-  \"$PROJECT\" \"$SELECT_SCRIPT\" \"$PWD/reascript/vgt_initialize.lua\" \"$SAVE_SCRIPT\"\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md:42:uv run python scripts/verify_phase0_apply.py \"$PROJECT\" \\\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-43-  --baseline \"test/Reaper Project/Reaper Project.RPP\"\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-44-```\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-45-\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md:46:REAPER runs command-line project and script arguments in order; its bundled Lua\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-47-runtime needs no external scripting dependency. The verifier reads only the\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-48-saved `.RPP` and its adjacent `.vgt` sidecar (named after the project, e.g.\ndocs/phase0-live-verification.md-49-`Reaper Project.vgt`). On each pass it checks:\n--\ndocs/GOAL.md-2-\ndocs/GOAL.md-3-## Background\ndocs/GOAL.md-4-We are building a (to some extent still experimental) virtual guitar teacher. We are at the initial phase.\ndocs/GOAL.md:5:The user interacts with vgt either from the command line or from the REAPER desktop app.\ndocs/GOAL.md-6-\ndocs/GOAL.md:7:In REAPER the user has typically one original song; in the future there is a possibility of having more than\ndocs/GOAL.md-8-one. That song may have adjusted pitch, be normalized, or have some marks — but otherwise it is not expected to\ndocs/GOAL.md-9-have significant modifications. This is the **reference song**.\ndocs/GOAL.md-10-\n--\ndocs/GOAL.md-28-conventions and the `<project>.vgt` sidecar established by the phases before it. \"Done\" for the whole project is\ndocs/GOAL.md-29-the union of every phase's deliverables below.\ndocs/GOAL.md-30-\ndocs/GOAL.md:31:### Phase 0 — REAPER-plumbing foundation *(delivered)*\ndocs/GOAL.md-32-\ndocs/GOAL.md:33:Establishes how vgt locates, opens, and safely augments a REAPER project, and where vgt stores its own state.\ndocs/GOAL.md-34-Shipped:\ndocs/GOAL.md:35:- **locating a REAPER project** — accept a project path as input;\ndocs/GOAL.md:36:- **defaulting to the current project** — when no project is given and there is already a REAPER project in the\ndocs/GOAL.md-37-  working directory, assume the user wants to work with that one;\ndocs/GOAL.md-38-- **opening the project and reading relevant information** — parse the project and read at least: sample rate,\ndocs/GOAL.md-39-  tempo/time-signature, and the existing track list (names and GUIDs) so vgt can tell which tracks are already\ndocs/GOAL.md-40-  vgt-managed;\ndocs/GOAL.md:41:- **storing vgt settings next to the REAPER project** — a single sidecar file living alongside the\ndocs/GOAL.md-42-  `.RPP` and sharing its name with a `.vgt` extension (e.g. `Reaper Project.RPP` → `Reaper Project.vgt`), holding\ndocs/GOAL.md-43-  vgt's own state (schema version, the vgt-managed track GUIDs it created, and any config);\ndocs/GOAL.md:44:- **preparing a \"vgt managed\" area for practice tracks** — a REAPER track folder (and a `[vgt]` name prefix on\ndocs/GOAL.md-45-  every track/region vgt creates) so a student can immediately identify what vgt owns;\ndocs/GOAL.md-46-- **choosing a reference track and mirroring it** — the apply action asks which of the project's own tracks is\ndocs/GOAL.md-47-  the reference, names the managed folder after it (`[vgt] <reference track name>`), and mirrors that one track's\n--\ndocs/GOAL.md-57-- **chords** — beat-aligned chord labels (maj/min vocabulary).\ndocs/GOAL.md-58-\ndocs/GOAL.md-59-Phase 1 delivers:\ndocs/GOAL.md:60:- **an analysis stage in the CLI** — heavy analysis runs in the Python CLI, never inside REAPER; the ReaScript\ndocs/GOAL.md-61-  action passes the reference track's source-file path (already identified in phase 0) to the CLI;\ndocs/GOAL.md-62-- **persisting analysis in the sidecar** — extend the `<project>.vgt` schema (bump `schema_version`) to hold the\ndocs/GOAL.md-63-  detected tempo/key/sections/chords alongside phase 0's state;\n--\ndocs/GOAL.md-67-  - `[vgt]` **section regions** (intro/verse/chorus/…), renamable by the user;\ndocs/GOAL.md-68-  - a **muted, locked `[vgt] Chords`** track carrying the beat-aligned chord labels as text items.\ndocs/GOAL.md-69-\ndocs/GOAL.md:70:**Out of scope for phase 1** (deferred to later phases): stem separation, guitar MIDI reference generation,\ndocs/GOAL.md-71-practice controls (stem muting / looping / tempo-ramp), and performance scoring. The full song-prep pipeline in\ndocs/GOAL.md-72-[phase1-song-prep-plan.md](phase1-song-prep-plan.md) is the longer-range roadmap; phase 1 here is its analysis\ndocs/GOAL.md:73:slice with separation and the MIDI reference intentionally deferred.\ndocs/GOAL.md-74-\ndocs/GOAL.md-75-## Requirements\ndocs/GOAL.md-76-\ndocs/GOAL.md-77-- **Non-destructive.** Never overwrite, rename, delete, or modify any track or object vgt did not create.\ndocs/GOAL.md-78-  Everything vgt creates carries the `[vgt]` prefix; only `[vgt]`-owned objects are ever touched. A re-apply\ndocs/GOAL.md-79-  first removes only its own `[vgt]` objects before recreating them.\ndocs/GOAL.md:80:- **Respect REAPER settings.** Open and read the REAPER project (and relevant preferences) and honor them —\ndocs/GOAL.md-81-  do not fight the project's existing sample rate, tempo, etc.\ndocs/GOAL.md:82:- **Prefer live, in-app manipulation.** The intended mechanism is to manipulate the project through the REAPER\ndocs/GOAL.md:83:  API via a ReaScript action (not by editing `.RPP` text), so the modifications appear immediately in the user's\ndocs/GOAL.md:84:  open project and REAPER handles project construction correctly. Where a piece is not yet technically feasible\ndocs/GOAL.md-85-  in-app, note it explicitly rather than silently falling back to text edits.\ndocs/GOAL.md:86:- **Analysis stays out of the DAW process.** ML/DSP analysis runs in the Python CLI; REAPER only reads the\ndocs/GOAL.md-87-  reference source path and applies results through the API. The DAW never loads heavy analysis dependencies.\ndocs/GOAL.md-88-- **Correctable.** Auto-analysis will sometimes be wrong; every detected value (tempo, key, sections, chords) is\ndocs/GOAL.md-89-  overridable, and corrections survive re-runs of the analysis.\ndocs/GOAL.md-90-- **CLI-initiated.** A command-line interface initiates the actions, with the intent that it be invoked from a\ndocs/GOAL.md:91:  ReaScript action inside REAPER. The ReaScript wrapper is a thin caller; the CLI does the work.\ndocs/GOAL.md-92-- **Idempotent.** Running the same command twice produces the same result — no duplicate tracks, folders, regions,\ndocs/GOAL.md-93-  or settings. Re-running reconciles vgt's managed area to the intended state.\ndocs/GOAL.md-94-- **Documented.** There is documentation describing how vgt works and how to run it.\n# Phase 0 live REAPER verification\n\nThis procedure proves the persisted result of the ReaScript, rather than only\ninspecting its Lua source. It uses a disposable copy and leaves the committed\nfixture untouched.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n- macOS with REAPER 7.x at `/Applications/REAPER.app`.\n- Python 3.11 and `uv`.\n- REAPER may already be open; the command below starts an isolated REAPER\n  instance with `-newinst`.\n\n## Run\n\nFrom the repository root, make a temporary copy that preserves the relative\n`Media/` directory. The helper save script is deliberately outside the repo;\nit makes REAPER save after the apply action.\n\nThe apply action prompts for a reference track. To keep the run non-interactive,\na `select.lua` runs first and sets the `vgt`/`reference_index` ExtState (0-based\nover the project's non-`[vgt]` tracks); index `1` is the first song track after\n`Click`.\n\n```sh\nRUN_DIR=\"$(mktemp -d /tmp/vgt-phase0.XXXXXX)\"\ncp -R \"test/Reaper Project\" \"$RUN_DIR/Reaper Project\"\nPROJECT=\"$RUN_DIR/Reaper Project/Reaper Project.RPP\"\nSELECT_SCRIPT=\"$RUN_DIR/select.lua\"\nSAVE_SCRIPT=\"$RUN_DIR/save.lua\"\nprintf '%s\\n' 'reaper.SetExtState(\"vgt\", \"reference_index\", \"1\", false)' > \"$SELECT_SCRIPT\"\nprintf '%s\\n' 'reaper.Main_SaveProject(0, false)' > \"$SAVE_SCRIPT\"\n\n/Applications/REAPER.app/Contents/MacOS/REAPER -newinst \\\n  \"$PROJECT\" \"$SELECT_SCRIPT\" \"$PWD/reascript/vgt_initialize.lua\" \"$SAVE_SCRIPT\"\nuv run python scripts/verify_phase0_apply.py \"$PROJECT\" \\\n  --baseline \"test/Reaper Project/Reaper Project.RPP\"\n\n# Repeat the exact select/apply/save sequence, then check it again for idempotency.\n/Applications/REAPER.app/Contents/MacOS/REAPER -newinst \\\n  \"$PROJECT\" \"$SELECT_SCRIPT\" \"$PWD/reascript/vgt_initialize.lua\" \"$SAVE_SCRIPT\"\nuv run python scripts/verify_phase0_apply.py \"$PROJECT\" \\\n  --baseline \"test/Reaper Project/Reaper Project.RPP\"\n```\n\nREAPER runs command-line project and script arguments in order; its bundled Lua\nruntime needs no external scripting dependency. The verifier reads only the\nsaved `.RPP` and its adjacent `.vgt` sidecar (named after the project, e.g.\n`Reaper Project.vgt`). On each pass it checks:\n\n- the original name/GUID pairs and project sample rate, tempo, and time\n  signature match the baseline;\n- the sidecar has schema version 1, exactly two distinct managed GUIDs, and a\n  config whose `reference_track_guid`/`reference_track_name` is one of the\n  original tracks and whose `folder_name` is `[vgt] <reference track name>`;\n- the sidecar GUIDs are exactly the two `[vgt]` tracks: the reference-named\n  folder with depth `+1`, followed by one `[vgt] Mirror` child with depth `-1`;\n- the mirror has exactly the chosen reference track's file-backed media items,\n  all of which resolve on disk. Thus the second pass also rejects duplicate\n  managed tracks or items.\n\n## Recorded run\n\n_Pending re-run after the reference-track-selection change (2026-07-18)._ The\nprior recorded run predates both the interactive reference picker and the\n`test/Reaper Project` fixture, so its numbers no longer describe this procedure\nand must be regenerated by running the commands above.\n# vgt\n\nPhase 0 of virtual guitar teacher: safe REAPER project plumbing. It reads an RPP project from the command line and uses a REAPER ReaScript action to add a small, vgt-owned practice area.\n\n## Install and inspect\n\nWith Python 3.11 and `uv`:\n\n```sh\nuv tool install .\nvgt \"test/Reaper Project/Reaper Project.RPP\"\n```\n\n`vgt [project.rpp]` (or the explicit `vgt inspect [project.rpp]`) accepts an explicit `.RPP` path. Without one it selects the only `.RPP` in the current directory; it refuses to guess if there are zero or multiple projects. It reports the project's sample rate, tempo/time signature, and track names/GUIDs.\n\n## Apply inside REAPER\n\nOpen and save the target project in REAPER 7.x. In **Actions → Show action list → ReaScript: Load**, load [reascript/vgt_initialize.lua](reascript/vgt_initialize.lua), then run it.\n\nThe action is intentionally the mutation path: it uses REAPER's API and never text-edits an RPP. It:\n\n- pops up a menu of the project's own (non-`[vgt]`) tracks and asks which one is the **reference** to mirror;\n- creates a REAPER folder named `[vgt] <reference track name>` (e.g. `[vgt] The Seven Rivers (Full March - 3_00)`) with a `[vgt] Mirror` child;\n- clones only the chosen reference track's file-backed media to the mirror track, without adding sends, changing source tracks, or changing project tempo/sample rate;\n- writes the adjacent `.vgt` sidecar — named after the project, e.g. `Reaper Project.vgt` — with its two created track GUIDs and config recording the reference track;\n- on re-apply, deletes only tracks whose GUID occurs in the sidecar **and** whose current name begins `[vgt]`, then recreates the same area, carrying forward any `analysis` block `vgt analyze` had already written (schema stays version 1 until analysis exists, then becomes version 2).\n\nThe folder/mirror are therefore idempotent and original tracks remain untouched. REAPER-native click-source items have no file to clone, so choosing a click track mirrors nothing. `vgt apply` validates the project path and directs you to this action rather than silently falling back to unsafe RPP editing.\n\nAutomation can skip the menu by setting the `vgt`/`reference_index` ExtState (a 0-based index over the non-`[vgt]` tracks) before running the action; interactive users are always prompted.\n\nUse a copy of any project when experimenting; the included `test/Reaper Project` fixture is read-mostly.\n\nFor a repeatable live-REAPER run against a disposable copy of that fixture, see\n[Phase 0 live verification](docs/phase0-live-verification.md). The verifier\nchecks the saved RPP and sidecar after both the first apply and re-apply.\n\n## Analyze the reference track (Phase 1)\n\n`vgt analyze [project.rpp]` requires a `.vgt` sidecar already written by the\napply action above. It resolves the reference track's source audio file,\nruns the tempo/key/sections/chords detectors, and writes the results back\ninto the sidecar as schema version 2 — never inside REAPER itself.\n\nEach detector's output is cached against a hash of the source audio and the\ndetector's settings, so re-running only recomputes stages whose inputs\nchanged. Any stage can be corrected by hand-editing its `value` in the\nsidecar and setting `human_verified: true`; `vgt analyze` then leaves that\nstage untouched on every later run, regardless of what the audio hash says.\n\n### Tempo & beat/downbeat grid\n\nThe `tempo` stage detects BPM, downbeat offset, and time signature from the\nreference track's full mix, and fits either a single constant tempo or a\npiecewise-linear tempo map (mode + residual are both recorded):\n\n- **Primary backend: madmom**'s DBN beat + downbeat trackers, installed via\n  the optional `madmom` extra (`uv sync --extra madmom`). madmom's last\n  release predates modern Python/NumPy and its build is fragile (Cython,\n  a legacy NumPy ABI, a deprecated `pkg_resources` import) — it is isolated\n  behind this extra precisely so a default install never needs it.\n- **Fallback: librosa's `beat_track`** (installed by default) when madmom\n  isn't installed, or fails to import. It gives beat times but no\n  downbeats, so time signature falls back to a `time_signature_hint`\n  setting (or `4/4`) rather than a detected bar length.\n\nEvery run also renders a **click-over-mix artifact** — `<project>.vgt-tempo-click.wav`,\nnext to the sidecar — so the detected grid can be checked by ear; it is\nvgt-owned and regenerated each run, not committed alongside the project.\nThe tempo stage's `value` also stores the raw detected `beat_times` — the\nshared beat-synchronous grid the `chords` stage below aligns to.\n\n### Key & beat-aligned chords\n\nThe `key` stage detects root + scale (major/minor) from the reference\ntrack's full mix:\n\n- **Primary backend: Essentia**'s `KeyExtractor`, used only if `essentia` is\n  importable in the environment (Essentia wheels are fragile on Apple\n  Silicon, so it's never a hard dependency and has no `uv` extra of its own).\n- **Fallback: librosa chroma** correlated against the 24 Krumhansl–Schmuckler\n  major/minor key templates — always available.\n\nThe `chords` stage detects a beat-aligned maj/min chord sequence, with every\nsegment boundary snapped onto the `tempo` stage's shared `beat_times` grid\n(not a separately detected grid):\n\n- **Primary backend: madmom**'s CNN chord recognizer, via the same `madmom`\n  extra as the tempo stage.\n- **Fallback: Chordino**, via the `sonic-annotator` vamp-plugin host, when\n  `sonic-annotator` and the `nnls-chroma` plugin are installed system-side\n  (neither ships as a pip package, so there's no `uv` extra for this either).\n- **Last resort: a chroma + template classifier** (librosa chroma, the 24\n  maj/min triad templates, majority-vote smoothed across neighboring beats)\n  — always available, and what actually runs in most dev/CI environments\n  since neither of the above is typically installed.\n\nOnly major/minor triads are ever recognized — 7ths, sus, add9, etc. all\ncollapse to their nearest maj/min match — so every result is flagged\n`\"vocabulary\": \"maj_min\"`.\n\nEvery run also renders a **chord-sheet artifact** —\n`<project>.vgt-chords.txt`, next to the sidecar — a plain-text timestamp +\nchord-label listing for by-eye verification.\n\n### Sections\n\nThe `sections` stage detects song-structure boundaries + generic labels\n(\"A\", \"B\", …) from the reference track's full mix, stored in the sidecar as\n`{\"index\", \"start_seconds\", \"end_seconds\", \"label\", \"backend\"}` entries\nspanning the track end-to-end:\n\n- **Primary backend: MSAF**'s novelty/structure segmentation, installed via\n  the optional `msaf` extra (`uv sync --extra msaf`). Like madmom above,\n  MSAF's maintenance is uncertain and its last release predates modern\n  Python/NumPy/SciPy — isolated behind this extra so a default install\n  never needs it.\n- **Fallback: a self-similarity novelty + peak-picking heuristic** (built on\n  librosa, installed by default) when MSAF isn't installed or fails to\n  process the track: a Foote-style checkerboard-kernel novelty curve over\n  chroma+MFCC features picks boundary candidates, then segments are\n  agglomeratively matched against earlier segments' feature centroids so a\n  recurring section (e.g. a chorus) gets the same label each time.\n\nLabels are intentionally generic — renaming them (`\"A\"` → `\"chorus\"`) is a\nnormal correction, not a failure, and (like every other stage) persists once\n`human_verified: true` is set. Every run also renders a **section-timeline\nartifact** — `<project>.vgt-sections.txt`, next to the sidecar — so the\ndetected structure can be checked by eye; it is vgt-owned and regenerated\neach run, not committed alongside the project.\n\n## Apply analysis (Phase 1)\n\nRun the same ReaScript again after `vgt analyze`. It reads the sidecar and adds\n`[vgt]` section regions plus a muted, locked `[vgt] Chords` track whose text\nitems carry the detected beat-aligned labels. It sets vgt-owned audio items to\ntime-based positioning before any tempo changes.\n\nWhen REAPER still has exactly its single default 120 BPM / 4/4 tempo marker,\nthe action writes the detected tempo map. If the project already has any other\ntempo map, it leaves that map untouched and instead creates a muted, locked\n`[vgt] Beats` item track. Both the regions and vgt tracks are reconciled on\nre-apply; no non-`[vgt]` track, region, or item is changed.\n\nFor a saved-project check after analysis, use:\n\n```sh\nuv run python scripts/verify_phase1_apply.py \"$PROJECT\" \\\n  --baseline \"test/Reaper Project/Reaper Project.RPP\"\n```\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
